          "type": "Exec"
        }
      ],
      "props": {
        "capabilities": [],
        "code": "let bound = 0;\nlet missing = [];\n\nfor (const element of document.querySelectorAll(\"[data-mcode-events]\")) {\n  for (const pair of element.getAttribute(\"data-mcode-events\").split(\",\")) {\n    const [event, name] = pair.split(\":\").map((part) => part.trim());\n    if (!event || !name) continue;\n\n    const handler = handlers[name];\n    if (typeof handler !== \"function\") {\n      // Another module on this page may own it. Only the last module to\n      // bind can tell that nobody does, so this is counted, not thrown.\n      if (!missing.includes(name)) missing.push(name);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const marker = \"mcodeBound\" + event + name;\n    if (element.dataset[marker]) continue;\n    element.dataset[marker] = \"1\";\n    element.addEventListener(event, handler);\n    bound += 1;\n  }\n}\n\nif (missing.length && globalThis.SOAR_DEBUG) {\n  console.warn(\"no handler bound for:\", missing.join(\", \"));\n}\nreturn bound;",
        "doc": "Attach every handler the markup declares.\n\nThe HTML backend stamps `data-mcode-events=\"click:onNavigate\"` onto the\nelement and expects something to honour it. Nothing did, so every screen\nrendered correctly and nothing on it responded - a whole workspace of live\nfunctions that no event ever reached.\n\nElements are marked once bound. A page that re-renders part of itself and\ncalls this again would otherwise stack a second listener on everything\nthat survived, and the second click would fire the handler twice.",
